[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Rolling Updates and Feature Flags in CI/CD Pipelines"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern software delivery, separating code deployment from feature release is essential for minimizing risk. This tutorial explores how to combine Kubernetes rolling updates with enterprise feature flag management platforms like LaunchDarkly and Unleash to achieve zero-downtime, risk-free continuous delivery."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Paradigm Shift"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traditional deployments couple releasing code with making features visible to end users. If a bug is found, the entire application must be rolled back. By decoupling these concepts using feature flags, you can deploy code to production safely behind a disabled flag, test it in production, and gradually roll it out to specific user segments."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rolling Updates: Gradually replaces old application pods with new ones without service interruption."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Feature Flags: Dynamically toggle features on or off remotely without redeploying code."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Targeted Rollouts: Expose new functionality to internal testers, beta users, or a percentage of total traffic."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Kubernetes Rolling Update Configuration"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To ensure continuous availability during a deployment, configure your Kubernetes Deployment manifest with appropriate maxSurge and maxUnavailable strategies. Here is a production-ready deployment configuration."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: payment-service
spec:
  replicas: 4
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1
      maxUnavailable: 0
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: payment-service
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: payment-service
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: app
        image: mycompany/payment-service:v2.0.0
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating Feature Flags with LaunchDarkly"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"LaunchDarkly provides robust SDKs to evaluate feature flags in real-time. When deploying applications via CI/CD, ensure that your application gracefully handles flag evaluations even if the service starts before the flag cache is fully synchronized."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const LaunchDarkly = require('launchdarkly-node-sdk');

const ldClient = LaunchDarkly.init(process.env.LD_SDK_KEY);

async function evaluateCheckoutFeature(user) {
  await ldClient.waitForInitialization();
  const showNewCheckout = await ldClient.variation('new-checkout-flow', user, false);

  if (showNewCheckout) {
    return renderNewCheckout(user);
  } else {
    return renderLegacyCheckout(user);
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating Feature Flags with Unleash"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unleash is an open-source alternative that offers bothhosted and self-managed feature flag architectures. The Unleash client periodically fetches toggle configurations from the Unleash API server, making evaluations extremely fast and resilient against network partitions."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"const { initialize, isEnabled } = require('unleash-client');

const unleash = initialize({
  url: 'https://unleash.herokuapp.com/api/',
  appName: 'payment-service',
  instanceId: 'instance-1',
  customHeaders: { Authorization: process.env.UNLEASH_API_TOKEN }
});

unleash.on('synchronized', () => {
  console.log('Unleash client synchronized with server');
});

function checkPaymentGateway(userContext) {
  return isEnabled('optimized-gateway', userContext);
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"CI/CD Pipeline Integration Workflow"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"A standard CI/CD workflow incorporating rolling updates and feature flags follows a strict sequential process. First, code is merged and built into a container image. Second, automated tests run against the image. Third, a Kubernetes rolling update deploys the new pods. Finally, the feature flag is enabled via API or Terraform after validation."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 1: Code Build and Unit Testing in GitHub Actions or GitLab CI."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 2: Container Push to Artifact Registry."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 3: Apply Kubernetes Deployment manifests to trigger the rolling update."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 4: Update feature flag state in LaunchDarkly or Unleash to 10% rollout."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Best Practices"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Kubernetes rolling updates with feature flag solutions like LaunchDarkly and Unleash, engineering teams achieve ultimate control over production releases. Always remember to clean up stale feature flags from your codebase once a feature reaches 100 percent rollout to prevent technical debt."}]
